![high](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/high-priority.svg)

The `validateBoundary` function acts as a validation script but does not defensively guard against missing, null, or non-object inputs, nor does it handle malformed nested properties (like `boundary.baseline_counts` or `boundary.source_context.source_artifacts`) gracefully. If any of these are nullish or malformed, the validator will throw an unhandled `TypeError` and crash instead of failing closed.

To ensure robustness and fail-closed behavior, we should explicitly validate the `boundary` input and use defensive guards when dereferencing and mapping nested properties.

```javascript
function validateBoundary(boundary) {
  if (!isObject(boundary)) {
    return {
      schema_version: 'semantic_kg_codex_native_content_boundary_validator_result.v1',
      generated_at: DEFAULT_GENERATED_AT,
      generated_by: SCRIPT_NAME,
      issue: ISSUE,
      parent_issue: PARENT_ISSUE,
      validator_status: 'fail',
      summary: {
        validation_status: 'fail',
        check_count: 1,
        check_pass_count: 0,
        check_fail_count: 1,
        source_artifact_count: 0,
        missing_source_artifact_count: 0,
        represented_row_count: 0,
        accepted_count: 0,
        provisional_safe_count: 0,
        review_required_count: 0,
        rejected_count: 0,
        blocked_quarantined_count: 0,
        external_model_call_count: 0,
        v1_safe_target_predicate_count: 0,
        high_risk_exclusion_count: 0,
        downstream_issue_count: 0,
      },
      checks: [
        failCheck('boundary_record_required', 'boundary_record_required_or_invalid')
      ],
    };
  }

  const counts = isObject(boundary.baseline_counts) ? boundary.baseline_counts : {};
  const sourceArtifacts = Array.isArray(boundary.source_context?.source_artifacts)
    ? boundary.source_context.source_artifacts
    : [];
  const missingArtifacts = sourceArtifacts.filter((artifact) => !artifact?.exists);
  const safePredicates = Array.isArray(boundary.v1_safe_target_predicates)
    ? boundary.v1_safe_target_predicates.filter(isObject).map((entry) => entry.predicate)
    : [];
  const highRiskPredicates = Array.isArray(boundary.high_risk_exclusions)
    ? boundary.high_risk_exclusions.filter(isObject).map((entry) => entry.predicate)
    : [];
  const expectedSafePredicates = V1_SAFE_TARGET_PREDICATES.map(([predicate]) => predicate);
  const expectedHighRiskPredicates = HIGH_RISK_EXCLUSIONS.map(([predicate]) => predicate);
  const issueOrder = Array.isArray(boundary.downstream_issue_order)
    ? boundary.downstream_issue_order.filter(isObject).map((entry) => entry.issue_number)
    : [];
  const expectedIssueOrder = DOWNSTREAM_ISSUE_ORDER.map(([issueNumber]) => issueNumber);
  const forbiddenMutationClean =
    counts.production_rag_reindex_count === 0 &&
    counts.vector_store_mutation_count === 0 &&
    counts.runtime_api_integration_count === 0 &&
    counts.frontend_integration_count === 0 &&
    boundary.execution_receipt?.production_rag_reindex_performed === false &&
    boundary.execution_receipt?.vector_store_mutation_performed === false &&
    boundary.execution_receipt?.remote_supabase_mutation_performed === false &&
    boundary.execution_receipt?.frontend_or_runtime_api_integration_performed === false;
```

<details>
<summary>References</summary>

1. Validation functions should explicitly handle missing, null, or non-object inputs by returning a specific error code (e.g., 'taxonomy_record_required') and failing closed, rather than silently returning empty failures or throwing a TypeError.
2. Ensure validation and check scripts handle missing or malformed critical artifacts gracefully without throwing unhandled exceptions. They should fail-closed by returning a failed validation status, a failure count, and diagnostic codes, backed by regression tests.
3. When filtering or mapping arrays of objects, use defensive guards (such as checking if the element is a valid object) to ignore nullish or malformed elements instead of throwing a TypeError.
</details>

![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)

The utility function `renderPredicateRows` maps over `entries` directly. If `entries` is not an array or contains nullish/malformed elements, it will throw a `TypeError`. We should ensure this helper is robust against non-array inputs and filters out non-object elements to fail closed gracefully.

```suggestion
function renderPredicateRows(entries) {
  if (!Array.isArray(entries)) return '';
  return entries.filter(isObject).map((entry) => (
    '| ' + entry.predicate + ' | ' + entry.risk_tier + ' | ' + (entry.target_v1_statuses?.join(', ') ?? entry.max_v1_auto_status) + ' | ' + (entry.rule ?? entry.reason) + ' |'
  )).join('\n');
}
```

<details>
<summary>References</summary>

1. Ensure utility functions (such as sorting, filtering, or mapping helpers) are robust against non-array inputs by returning a safe default (e.g., an empty array) to fail closed gracefully instead of throwing a TypeError.
2. When filtering or mapping arrays of objects, use defensive guards (such as checking if the element is a valid object) to ignore nullish or malformed elements instead of throwing a TypeError.
</details>
